1
00:00:05,410 --> 00:01:23,520
 that's life. Allow us to enjoy the magic of music. What happens when we hear an instrument played. What happens is that waves are created in the air that surrounds us. And these expanding ripples of pressure are the sound detected by our ears.

2
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:35,470
 This makes the eardrum vibrate.

3
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:40,870
 The eardrum, in turn, transmits the signal to a tiny bone called the hammer,

4
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:44,110
 which beats on another bone known as the anvil.

5
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:52,500
 Third bone, the stirrup, passes on the amplified vibrations to the inner ear.

6
00:01:55,100 --> 00:02:01,200
 Here we find the cochlea and these ultra-sensitive hairs, the corti organs,

7
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:03,630
 which tremble with every vibration.

8
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:07,720
 Sodium-ion messengers swim about in a state of readiness.

9
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:11,340
 Inside the cell, there is much activity.

10
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:15,080
 Just like the normal hustle and bustle we've seen in other cells.

11
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:23,840
 When we penetrate the nucleus, we find a control room devoted to the measurement of sound.

12
00:02:24,830 --> 00:02:29,190
 These are medium-loud vibrations of about 80 decibels.

13
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:49,110
 Let's get them recorded, polarize the cell, let the sodium ion messengers in.

14
00:02:49,950 --> 00:02:51,710
 They'll pass the signal on for us.

15
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:52,700
 Huh?

16
00:02:53,120 --> 00:03:00,150
 I can't hear anything. They told me I was assigned to a sound transmission unit.

17
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:02,300
 That's absolutely right.

18
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,370
 Why is it so quiet then?

19
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:06,510
 Because we don't hear anything, you see.

20
00:03:06,830 --> 00:03:08,550
 Our job is transmitting vibrations.

21
00:03:08,830 --> 00:03:10,830
 Their frequency, their amplitude, that's it.

22
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:12,420
 I think I see what you mean.

23
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:16,120
 I suppose it must be the brain that turns the vibrations into sound.

24
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:17,780
 Yes, that's where they do the hearing.

25
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:21,890
 The screen. You can see the walls of the cell out there.

26
00:03:22,170 --> 00:03:23,930
 Watch for the sodium ion messengers.

27
00:03:24,270 --> 00:03:26,290
 Their job is to help the neurotransmitters.

28
00:03:26,710 --> 00:03:33,890
 All right, where to now?

29
00:03:34,110 --> 00:03:35,070
 Thought you and you.

30
00:03:35,070 --> 00:03:38,100
 What a shambles. Who's in charge?

31
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:39,950
 I've got it, guys.

32
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:42,700
 The neurotransmitters are this way.

33
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:44,470
 This way!

34
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:54,110
 Come on, push, push harder!

35
00:03:54,110 --> 00:03:58,530
 Some sodium ions don't pull their weight.

36
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:00,500
 I'm not one of them.

37
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:02,910
 Why don't you save your breath for pushing?

38
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:10,000
 Chaps, we're almost there!

39
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 Unload the neurotransmitters for the auditory nerve!

40
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:27,790
 That's how it's done.

41
00:04:27,790 --> 00:04:33,820
 First of all, we register the vibrations, then pass them on as auditory nerve impulses to the brain.

42
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:35,000
 So that's hearing, is it?

43
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,260
 Not by a long joke, my lad.

44
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:41,180
 You see, the signals we send can only be interpreted by the brain itself.

45
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:45,540
 And the messages we transmit have still got an awful long way to travel.

46
00:04:49,090 --> 00:04:54,810
 Messages carried along the nerve cell until it reaches the nucleus with a control room very similar to our own.

47
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:59,530
 And when things are quiet outside, they're quiet inside it as well.

48
00:05:21,870 --> 00:05:35,790
 This harmonic wave is only 65 decibels.

49
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:42,450
 P.D., your job is delivering messages, not reading them.

50
00:05:42,750 --> 00:05:44,530
 Now take it to the eighth auditory nerve.

51
00:05:45,820 --> 00:06:23,700
 In the hurry, she'll be put away for life.

52
00:06:30,670 --> 00:06:46,890
 Message coming through.

53
00:06:56,700 --> 00:07:06,550
 Turns up the nerve cell until it has to jump to the next cell, the junction.

54
00:07:08,210 --> 00:07:11,890
 See the message relayed from cell to cell along the auditory nerve.

55
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:18,720
 Finally, the impulse reaches the brain, where it's converted into the sound we hear.

56
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:41,470
 Groove, daddy-o, in the groove. Wonderful, isn't it?

57
00:07:42,140 --> 00:07:45,210
 Simple sound vibrations, that's all they are.

58
00:07:45,210 --> 00:07:50,530
 But when the ear has picked them up and the brain has processed them, they're pure magic.

59
00:07:50,790 --> 00:07:54,330
 How the brain does it is a bit of a mystery, but what a marvel all the same.

60
00:08:28,910 --> 00:08:46,960
 They say that music comes a savage breast, but personally I find it rather stimulating.

61
00:09:36,520 --> 00:10:10,590
 Lobus lost your sea legs.

62
00:10:12,200 --> 00:10:13,370
 Something bothering you, Liv.

63
00:10:14,530 --> 00:10:17,060
 There's something very wrong in this section, Captain.

64
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:19,030
 Well, your hunches are usually right.

65
00:10:19,170 --> 00:10:20,070
 Lead the way, Lieutenant.

66
00:10:25,490 --> 00:10:29,480
 That earthquake.

67
00:10:30,530 --> 00:10:31,450
 A drum, you see.

68
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:35,800
 Three little bones, the hammer, the anvil, and the stirrup.

69
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:41,640
 Maybe the smallest bones in the volley, but they amplify the vibrations two times.

70
00:10:48,550 --> 00:10:53,340
 Who's that?

71
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:59,030
 It's all very well, but here we are, climbing up a dark, slippery tunnel.

72
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:00,550
 We don't even know where we are.

73
00:11:00,610 --> 00:11:02,410
 I know where we are, numskull.

74
00:11:02,630 --> 00:11:04,030
 In the Eustachian tube.

75
00:11:04,210 --> 00:11:06,780
 It links the nose to the ear, which is where we're headed.

76
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:08,280
 Why the ear, huh?

77
00:11:08,870 --> 00:11:10,980
 I tell you why.

78
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:14,300
 It's a nice, quiet place where we can creep in undetected.

79
00:11:15,670 --> 00:11:24,350
 To have a leader with brains like me, we're going to set up our beautiful otitis.

80
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:25,600
 What's that?

81
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:29,000
 You ignorant slob, you don't even know what otitis is.

82
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:33,540
 Idiotitis is an ear infection that young kids get all the time.

83
00:11:33,880 --> 00:11:34,850
 Let's move along.

84
00:11:36,770 --> 00:11:37,580
 Hold it.

85
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,400
 I don't like the look of that.

86
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:40,780
 Bite yourselves.

87
00:11:44,350 --> 00:11:49,910
 Down there, Captain. Do you see them?

88
00:11:50,220 --> 00:11:51,970
 Yes, Lieutenant. Let's move in closer.

89
00:11:55,030 --> 00:11:57,760
 Calling all antibody troops.

90
00:11:58,580 --> 00:12:03,460
 Report immediately to right your station tube to repel large invasion force of Staphylococcus germs.

91
00:12:03,460 --> 00:12:09,400
 I repeat, report to right your station tube to repel large invasion force of Staphylococcus germs.

92
00:12:10,710 --> 00:12:14,720
 Message received, we're on our way, over and out.

93
00:12:20,220 --> 00:12:27,420
 We can keep it undetected, he said.

94
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:35,260
 Mission accomplished, Captain, no problem.

95
00:12:35,540 --> 00:12:38,540
 We're more than a match for those hulking great Staphylococcus thugs.

96
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:42,880
 Yes, indeed, Metro. It looks as though we've nipped that one in the bud.

97
00:12:43,780 --> 00:12:46,830
 Funny. I had a pain in my ear, but it's gone away now.

98
00:12:53,900 --> 00:13:02,760
 You seem to be getting on quite well with your piano lessons.

99
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,040
 Are you finished now?

100
00:13:04,970 --> 00:13:06,590
 We're riding our bikes.

101
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:10,120
 Good idea. We can take a ride over to the park and back again.

102
00:13:30,150 --> 00:13:40,110
 Droppy, there's no cops in sight.

103
00:13:40,350 --> 00:13:46,220
 They're himself killed.

104
00:14:04,140 --> 00:14:31,750
 What do you think you're flapping?

105
00:14:31,750 --> 00:14:35,770
 Because it's a very loud noise, 140 decibels, it's painful.

106
00:14:36,110 --> 00:14:37,510
 We'd better send it through urgently.

107
00:14:48,980 --> 00:14:59,790
 Use normal channels for 140 decibel noise like that.

108
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:02,700
 What we need is a priority pain messenger.

109
00:15:03,380 --> 00:15:04,080
 Thanks.

110
00:15:08,370 --> 00:15:16,070
 So many pain signals at once, it's abnormal.

111
00:15:16,670 --> 00:15:18,070
 Could there be some mistake?

112
00:15:18,420 --> 00:15:21,020
 Or do you think we should send a message to block the ears?

113
00:15:21,310 --> 00:15:23,230
 We can't take that sort of decision.

114
00:15:23,450 --> 00:15:24,890
 It has to be handled upstairs.

115
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:27,740
 So let's get on and transmit those pain messages.

116
00:15:28,300 --> 00:15:29,820
 Ours not to reason why, I'm afraid.

117
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:31,380
 Ours but to do our job.

118
00:15:31,540 --> 00:15:32,380
 So let's get to work.

119
00:15:35,130 --> 00:15:37,840
 Work like a slave and where does it get you, huh?

120
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,600
 While whippersnappers like him get promoted to the flying squad.

121
00:15:42,240 --> 00:15:44,070
 Question of fashion.

122
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:47,210
 Is it the in thing to wear leathers and a helmet these days?

123
00:15:47,560 --> 00:15:48,300
 That's it!

124
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:49,880
 Why on earth didn't I think of it earlier?

125
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:50,860
 Just watch me.

126
00:15:51,020 --> 00:15:53,200
 I'm about to join the very latest fashion trend.

127
00:15:56,330 --> 00:15:58,340
 Him, didn't I?

128
00:16:05,060 --> 00:16:22,530
 We'd be delighted to come forward as witnesses

129
00:16:22,530 --> 00:16:25,430
 if you decide to prosecute those reckless children.

130
00:16:25,970 --> 00:16:27,250
 The lights were red.

131
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:29,390
 Traffic lights were green, weren't they?

132
00:16:29,730 --> 00:16:31,530
 Well, maybe they were amber.

133
00:16:31,630 --> 00:16:32,730
 Don't you agree, Strobby?

134
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:33,930
 And that's not all.

135
00:16:34,010 --> 00:16:35,690
 Just how fast do you think you were going?

136
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:37,910
 Well, we weren't speeding.

137
00:16:38,350 --> 00:16:40,700
 We were doing 30 or 40 at the outside.

138
00:16:41,150 --> 00:16:43,630
 And your horn, it's a regulation hooter, I suppose,

139
00:16:43,850 --> 00:16:45,530
 even though it's still ringing in my ears.

140
00:16:46,870 --> 00:16:48,090
 Exceeding the speed limit,

141
00:16:48,330 --> 00:16:49,730
 failing to stop at a red light,

142
00:16:49,830 --> 00:16:51,610
 and using an illegal warning device.

143
00:16:52,330 --> 00:16:54,120
 Now, what about your license?

144
00:16:54,880 --> 00:16:56,330
 If you can't produce your license,

145
00:16:56,490 --> 00:16:57,450
 you're really in hot water.

146
00:17:23,930 --> 00:17:36,940
 Working down here, but now I can't stand it.

147
00:17:36,980 --> 00:17:38,060
 I just can't stand it.

148
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:39,580
 The noise is driving me mad.

149
00:17:39,580 --> 00:17:43,420
 I can't bear it much longer.

150
00:17:43,740 --> 00:17:44,580
 It's driving me crazy.

151
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:46,260
 The noise is going to destroy me.

152
00:17:46,720 --> 00:17:49,280
 Not 'til you've dealt with all these signals, young man.

153
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:59,200
 What's going on here, Chris?

154
00:17:59,200 --> 00:18:01,970
 Thank you. You'd best be on your way.

155
00:18:01,970 --> 00:18:04,250
 Oh, I'm not trying to make trouble, sir.

156
00:18:04,250 --> 00:18:08,030
 It's just that I don't understand what's happening.

157
00:18:08,030 --> 00:18:11,060
 My dear young chap, sounds are only vibrations.

158
00:18:11,060 --> 00:18:13,760
 And when the vibrations become too strong, they hurt.

159
00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:16,360
 The pain is meant as a danger signal to warn us.

160
00:18:16,360 --> 00:18:17,580
 A danger signal?

161
00:18:17,580 --> 00:18:20,470
 Yes, it all goes back to the times of the cavemen.

162
00:18:20,470 --> 00:18:24,320
 Of course, in those days, our hearing was much more acute.

163
00:18:29,120 --> 00:19:03,040
 Years ago, sound is still a warning to us in modern time.

164
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:07,550
 A phone could be a warning for...

165
00:19:07,550 --> 00:19:11,550
 The pain tells you that your hearing is in danger.

166
00:19:11,550 --> 00:19:29,760
 See, the receptor cells are fragile and noise can shatter them.

167
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:47,520
 Nowadays, we know how to protect ourselves against harmful noise.

168
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:51,390
 But some of us don't know just how harmful it can be.

169
00:19:59,420 --> 00:20:19,480
 Already, I'm afraid.

170
00:20:19,480 --> 00:20:23,130
 There's nothing we can do to bring it back and he'll have to be very careful in the future.

171
00:20:23,130 --> 00:20:24,730
 What can he do, Doctor?

172
00:20:24,730 --> 00:20:27,610
 Lower volume for shorter periods, that's my advice.

173
00:20:27,610 --> 00:20:31,740
 Too much noise causes stress and destroys the sensory cells in the ears.

174
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:33,260
 The damage can never be repaired.

175
00:20:33,540 --> 00:20:34,640
 The hearing loss is permanent.

176
00:20:35,180 --> 00:20:38,820
 Message for the nervous system, increase the rate of heartbeat immediately.

177
00:20:40,930 --> 00:20:44,070
 Message for the lymphatic nervous system, constrict the capillaries.

178
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:48,620
 Message for the endocrine glands, increase secretion of adrenaline with immediate effect.

179
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:50,410
 There you are.

180
00:20:50,840 --> 00:20:53,310
 Bye for now, young fellow. I'll talk to you again soon.

181
00:20:53,880 --> 00:20:56,480
 Do you see how too much noise can affect your health?

182
00:20:56,750 --> 00:20:59,120
 So do take good care of your ears.

183
00:20:59,120 --> 00:21:13,510
 I just want to lose their balance.

184
00:21:13,690 --> 00:21:14,510
 Okay, let's try.

185
00:21:19,800 --> 00:21:21,770
 Point to me.

186
00:21:25,090 --> 00:21:31,970
 Not so easy going slow and trying to keep your balance.

187
00:21:36,640 --> 00:21:40,880
 Do we keep...

188
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:43,430
 Well, here again, we use our ears.

189
00:21:43,860 --> 00:21:46,300
 It's our ears that help us maintain our balance.

190
00:21:46,710 --> 00:21:50,770
 Or at least a very special part of the ears called the vestibular apparatus.

191
00:21:51,570 --> 00:21:56,410
 Our old friend, Professor Globus, is taking some young corpuscles on a guided tour of the area.

192
00:21:57,230 --> 00:21:58,460
 Why don't we join them?

193
00:21:58,460 --> 00:22:01,370
 In fact, balance is a sense in its own right, isn't it?

194
00:22:01,770 --> 00:22:04,120
 And those are its semicircular canals.

195
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:14,100
 You see? How'd you like that?

196
00:22:15,820 --> 00:22:20,490
 As I was saying before...

197
00:22:20,490 --> 00:22:24,670
 What was that earthquake, professor?

198
00:22:24,670 --> 00:22:26,720
 Must have been a movement of the head. Look!

199
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:31,870
 The head moves. The sensory cells inside the canals can detect this movement.

200
00:22:31,870 --> 00:22:49,160
 The movement has been...

201
00:22:49,160 --> 00:22:52,210
 Head is tilted forward 15 degrees.

202
00:22:52,210 --> 00:23:12,560
 Amid sodium ion messengers to activate neurotransmittance.

203
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:39,940
 Port states that the field of vision has shifted lower down, sir.

204
00:23:39,940 --> 00:23:42,430
 I have a message from the neck muscles.

205
00:23:42,430 --> 00:23:43,970
 The head is tilting slightly.

206
00:23:43,970 --> 00:23:47,810
 According to this message, pressure is increasing on the left knee joint.

207
00:23:47,810 --> 00:24:01,980
 Why won't you go in?

208
00:24:01,980 --> 00:24:04,250
 Ah, that's better.

209
00:24:04,250 --> 00:24:18,510
 Chant signals from the semi-circular canals.

210
00:24:18,510 --> 00:24:22,750
 The head is tilted 15 degrees forward.

211
00:24:22,750 --> 00:24:27,970
 I think he ought to turn the handlebars.

212
00:24:27,970 --> 00:24:30,110
 He should push down on the pedal instead.

213
00:24:30,110 --> 00:24:32,450
 In my opinion, he should shift forward in the saddle.

214
00:24:32,450 --> 00:24:34,750
 Now, I don't agree with that at all, Chief.

215
00:24:34,750 --> 00:24:36,640
 Enough of this chatter.

216
00:24:36,640 --> 00:24:39,040
 Our job is to send those signals upstairs.

217
00:24:39,040 --> 00:24:42,110
 He'll fall off his bike if we fall behind with our work.

218
00:24:42,110 --> 00:24:44,900
 How moved!

219
00:24:44,900 --> 00:24:50,490
 The organ of hearing, but as we've just seen, that's not all.

220
00:24:50,490 --> 00:24:55,640
 With the vestibular apparatus and its semicircular canals, the ear is also the organ of balance.

221
00:24:55,640 --> 00:24:58,970
 Without our ears, we could never stand up, let alone ride a bike.

